# Time & Time Travel In A Text-Based RPG

## Scope

This essay discusses both time & time travel within a text-based RPG. Its primary focus is on digital games that emulate traditional table-top RPGs; specifically, its focus is on LLM driven games, though much of it could apply to more traditional MUD-like platforms.

The essay is split into two chapters, as well as this introduction, and a [research document](./99-research-scratchpad) that currently functions as a scratchpad for writing, thoughts, and conversations related to the topic.

### Chapter 1: The Passage of Time

This chapter primarily details the challenges of and approaches to handling [the passage of time](./01-passage-of-time) in a text-based rpg, in which advancement may be non-linear, and in fact may be non-linear for multiple characters.

It also presents approaches to passing time when many players are present, such as in an MMO setting.

The questions raised in this chapter, while not necessarily straightforward, have more or less been solved before; however, they raise questions that *must* be answered when building out a text-based RPG.

### Chapter 2: Time Travel

This essay, in my opinion, is the much more interesting of the two. It discusses a much different way of advancing through time: Traveling backwards. It proposes a mechanism for doing so that avoids many of the common pitfalls and paradoxes involved in [time travel](./02-time-travel): Branching upon causal divergence. It approaches the topic from an analytical and philosophical point of view. It presents a general technical implementation guide, though exact implementation details are deferred to a future technical document.

